---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { links, arxiv, riddles, project, paperLab } from '../data/site';

const knobs = [
  { k: 'Learning rule', d: 'Backprop versus local and biologically inspired alternatives.', now: false },
  { k: 'Architecture', d: 'Symmetry and equivariance built into the network.', now: false },
  { k: 'Objective', d: 'Predictive world models: physical state or surface statistics?', now: false },
  { k: 'Memory', d: 'What must a world model keep to act well, and what does keeping it cost?', now: true },
];
---
<Base title="Research" description="Keepsake, a study of lean memory for real-time world-model planning, and CulturalRiddles, a multicultural riddles benchmark.">
  <header class="page-head narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
    <h1>When a model succeeds, has it learned the structure, or the surface cues?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One question runs through the work: what a model actually learns, and what it needs to keep,
      when you constrain how it learns. I answer it with controlled environments, matched baselines,
      probes and causal tests rather than leaderboard scores.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="narrow program reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The programme · one question, four knobs, one toolkit</p>
    <div class="knobs">
      {knobs.map((x) => (
        <div class:list={['knob', { now: x.now }]}>
          <span class="mono label">{x.now ? 'now' : 'later'}</span>
          <h3>{x.k}</h3>
          <p>{x.d}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="meta tools">Instruments held constant: environments with ground-truth state · matched baselines and ablations · linear and nonlinear probing · activation patching · hypotheses stated before results, negative results reported</p>
  </section>

  <article id="keepsake" class="narrow paper reveal">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="pill live">In progress</span>
      <span class="pill">World models · robot learning</span>
    </div>
    <h2>{project.name}</h2>
    <p class="subtitle">{project.question}</p>
    <p class="venue mono">Working title: {project.paperTitle} · preprint planned for December 2026</p>

    <h4 class="mono">Abstract</h4>
    <p>
      Most robot foundation models have no memory: they act on the current camera frame alone. That
      works until the frame stops telling you how far along you are: identical objects, a cue that has
      disappeared, something hidden from view. Giving a model memory the obvious way is expensive. Ten
      seconds of video from four cameras at 50 Hz is about half a million tokens.
    </p>
    <p>
      {project.name} tests a lean alternative inside a latent world-model planner. The planner runs
      without memory by default. When its own one-step prediction turns out wrong, it writes that moment
      into a small, fixed-size memory, and plans with it. Surprise is treated as a hypothesis, not a
      definition of importance: it may miss progress the robot caused itself, and fire on changes that
      do not matter. On tasks built so that the current frame is not enough, it is compared against no
      memory, frame stacking, a recurrent state, full history and an oracle given the true task state.
      Two claims are tested separately: whether a compact memory is worth its cost, and whether surprise
      is a good way to choose what goes into it.
    </p>
    <p class="prior">
      Closest prior work gates episodic memory on surprise to help a language-model agent answer questions
      about what a robot saw, and does not report timing. {project.name} uses the planner's own
      action-conditioned prediction error, puts the memory inside the control loop, and treats speed as a
      primary result.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">JEPA world model · latent planning · event memory · simulation first, then a real arm</p>
    <p class="note">The animation on the <a href="/">home page</a> sketches the idea. It is an illustration, not model output.</p>
  </article>

  <article id="culturalriddles" class="narrow paper reveal">
    <div class="tags">
      <span class="pill live">MRL 2026</span>
      <span class="pill">Co-first author</span>
      <span class="pill">Multilingual evaluation</span>
    </div>
    <h2>CulturalRiddles</h2>
    <p class="subtitle">A multicultural benchmark of riddles across 51 languages.</p>

    <p class="venue mono">
      {arxiv.url ? <a href={arxiv.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv{arxiv.id ? `:${arxiv.id}` : ' preprint'} ↗</a> : 'arXiv preprint (Oct 2026)'}
      · To be presented at MRL 2026, Budapest · under review at TACL
    </p>

    <p>
      Riddles are a setting where surface fluency and real understanding come apart sharply. A model
      can produce perfect Tamil and still miss what a Tamil riddle is actually about. CulturalRiddles
      collects around 6,100 riddles written by native speakers from 62 language communities, and
      evaluates how well language models solve them in the cultural context they come from.
    </p>
    <ul class="role">
      <li>Co-first author and cross-team lead, with the Cohere Labs Open Science Community</li>
      <li>Led data creation, validation, evaluation and analysis</li>
      <li>Coordinated 200+ contributors across writing, validation and evaluation, including 165 riddle writers</li>
      <li>A living dataset: new communities keep joining</li>
      <li>Designed the canonical schema, the benchmarking pipeline and the dataset release</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="links">
      <a class="chip" href={arxiv.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paper ↗</a>
      <a class="chip" href={riddles.blog} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Research blog ↗</a>
      <a class="chip" href={riddles.dashboard} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dashboard ↗</a>
      <a class="chip" href={riddles.dataset} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dataset ↗</a>
      <a class="chip" href={riddles.quiz} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Take the quiz ↗</a>
    </p>

    <div class="piece">
      <p class="eyebrow">I built · results dashboard</p>
      <h3>The CulturalRiddles dashboard</h3>
      <p>
        A public dashboard on Hugging Face Spaces for exploring the benchmark by model, culture, region,
        difficulty and error type. Every figure is reconciled to the human-evaluation source data.
      </p>
      <div class="embed">
        <iframe src={riddles.dashboardEmbed} title="CulturalRiddles results dashboard" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <a class="btn" href={riddles.dashboard} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the dashboard <span class="arrow">↗</span></a>
    </div>

    <div class="piece">
      <p class="eyebrow">I co-wrote · Cohere Labs research blog</p>
      <h3><a href={riddles.blog} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Where models fall short</a></h3>
      <p>
        The public write-up of the paper: why a 79% overall score hides communities where no model
        passes 22%, why translation only closes part of the gap, and why models reach for the familiar
        answer. Co-written with Tianyi Hu, Madeline Smith and Julia Kreutzer.
      </p>
      <a class="btn" href={riddles.blog} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the blog <span class="arrow">↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="narrow reveal next">
    <p>
      The <a href="/lab">Lab</a> is where {project.name} gets built, one step at a time: {paperLab.length} experiments
      toward the paper, and more after it. Follow along on <a href={links.substack} target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Meta Gradient</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .program { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .knobs { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: .8rem; }
  .knob { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 14px; padding: 1.1rem 1.1rem 1rem; background: var(--bg-raised); }
  .knob.now { border-color: var(--accent); box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--accent-soft); }
  .knob h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: .4rem 0 .35rem; }
  .knob p { font-size: .95rem; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; }
  .label { font-size: .65rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .knob.now .label { color: var(--accent); }
  .tools { margin-top: 1rem; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .knobs { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .paper { padding: 3.5rem 0 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 3rem; scroll-margin-top: 80px; }
  .tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4rem; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .paper h2 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.7rem); }
  .subtitle { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 1.3rem; margin: .3rem 0 1.8rem; }
  h4.mono { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint); font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 .8rem; }
  .venue { font-size: .8rem; color: var(--accent); border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); padding: .2rem 0 .2rem .8rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .role { padding-left: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .role li { margin-bottom: .3rem; }
  .role li::marker { color: var(--accent); }
  .prior { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 1.02rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--rule); padding-left: .9rem; }
  .venue a { color: inherit; }
  .links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; }
  .chip { font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: .8rem; text-decoration: none; padding: .3em .8em; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 999px; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .chip:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .piece { margin-top: 2.6rem; padding-top: 1.8rem; border-top: 1px dashed var(--rule); }
  .piece h3 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  .piece h3 a { text-decoration: none; }
  .piece p { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .embed { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 14px; overflow: hidden; margin: 1rem 0 1.1rem; background: var(--bg-raised); }
  .embed iframe { display: block; width: 100%; height: 560px; border: 0; }
  .note { font-size: .95rem; color: var(--ink-soft); font-style: italic; }
  .next { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 2rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
